From: Wren <wren@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] WebCore MSE: keep a bounded amount of played media behind the playhead (plain MediaSource, GStreamer)

For a plain MediaSource WebKit evicts only when an append would exceed the maximum buffer size (hundreds of MB on this
port), and the memory-pressure eviction (MediaSource::memoryPressure) is for ManagedMediaSource only, which Wren hides
from pages. A long 4K MSE playback therefore keeps all the media it has played: on the TV the web process grew about
2.5 MB/s and was killed at the memory limit after 80 s.

SourceBufferPrivate::evictCodedFrames() (run before every append) now also queues evictBackBufferInternal() on the
dispatcher when the append fits: it removes the buffered media from the start up to min(currentTime - keep, the last
sync sample before currentTime), the same range rule as the first phase of evictFrames(), so the GOP being played and
everything ahead stay; computeEvictionData() follows. No updating/updateend events, no change to the byte limits, so
players see only a smaller buffered range behind the playhead (seeking back past it re-fetches).

keep comes from SourceBufferPrivate::platformMaximumBackBufferDuration(): invalid (off) in WebCore, and on the GStreamer
port MSE_MAX_BACK_BUFFER_SECONDS (default 30, minimum 3 when set, 0 = off).

Seek back out of the buffered media: the player re-fetches from the new position while what it had buffered ahead of the old
one stays far beyond the playhead, so the re-fetched segments came on top of it (about +90 MB at 4K on the TV). When the playhead
is found more than 5 s behind its position at the previous check and is not inside the buffered media, the data more than
`keep` seconds beyond it is removed too (never the range being played; a seek inside the buffer removes nothing).
